 ## Overview
-Computes the mean curvature $\kappa$ of a scalar field $f$ at every active voxel by evaluating the divergence of the normalized gradient field, $\kappa = \nabla \cdot (\nabla f / |\nabla f|)$ [S1, unofficial]. It distinguishes itself from sibling methods by combining first and second derivative computations into a single stencil pass, avoiding the creation of intermediate gradient or Hessian grids [S1, unofficial].
+Computes the mean curvature $\kappa$ of a scalar field $f$ at every active voxel by evaluating the divergence of the normalized gradient field, $\kappa = \nabla \cdot (\nabla f / |\nabla f|)$ [S1, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from sibling methods like [Laplacian Grid Computation](3160-laplacian-grid-computation.md) by normalizing the gradient vector before differentiation, thereby measuring the average of the principal curvatures rather than the sum of second derivatives [S1, unofficial]. This method is implemented as a single pass using a `BoxStencil`, avoiding the creation of intermediate gradient or Hessian grids [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
-**Inputs:** A scalar field grid $f$ defined on a voxel grid with coordinates $(x, y, z)$.
-**Outputs:** A grid of mean curvature values $\kappa$ with the same spatial layout as the input.
+**Inputs and Outputs:**
+- Input: A discrete grid tree representing a scalar field $f$.
+- Output: A new grid containing the mean curvature $\kappa$ at each active voxel, with a background value of zero.
 
 **Data Structures:**
-The method uses a `BoxStencil` object [S1, unofficial]. This stencil provides access to voxel values at relative offsets from a center coordinate. It encapsulates the logic for retrieving values at neighbors required for finite difference approximations.
